56":"d1dcc2c0170480867092de72e293df501cc78015228252e81620ede6b5ad6131","schema":"quartic-tie-review-v1","source_snapshot_sha256":"0d109ea9e9b7f4a766640d406409e30589fb4c5a4e645b6d6888569fa2edf29f","statement_id":"lem-2.2.i","statement_sha256":"f930e1d7cf9389128a673a84062b26f3748831797154a318e9ac129e667ad730"},"review_evidence":"current","reviewed_by":"ai:round3-construction-source-review","reviewed_relation":"stronger","reviewed_route":"same","route":"same","wrapper_comparison_evidence":"not_run"}],"original_statement_sha256":"9a4c83cc73c1f7a4a2114ba7d9af01dbf2dc153588d78e5bc5cf84ba0fc73322","passages":["CAT-lem-2.2.i"],"section":"2.2","semantic_probes":[],"source_line":266,"statement":"For every input y and (i,s) in [m] x {0,1}^r: i wins at s iff all K_{k,m} tests pass, namely (a) X_i[b]=1 for each b; (b) for each j != i, X_j[p_{i,s,j}]=0; (c) for each h and each non-input gate, the claimed value equals the gate function of its predecessors, read from the transcript or from A_{i,h}; (d) for each h, claimed d=1 and claimed b=s_h. Each test is computed by a deterministic adaptive bit-query decision tree of depth at most max(3,l+1), which is l+1 for m>=4: a pointer test reads l pointer bits, then the one target bit they select, while gate tests read at most three fixed bits. A pointer test is not a function of a small fixed set of bits: it can depend on all l+m pointer and target coordinates.","statement_sha256":"f930e1d7cf9389128a673a84062b26f3748831797154a318e9ac129e667ad730"}
